# Prove that activating one item out of a stack costs the stack nothing, and
# that the activation still does its work. This is the oracle for inc-od6j.
#
#   tools/headless.sh tools/keys/activate-stack.keys 4
#
# THE ITEM IS A HORN OF PLENTY AND THE CHOICE IS DELIBERATE. Three things had
# to be true of it at once. It stacks: it is a T_TOOL, and Item::operator==
# (src/Item.cpp) lists T_TOOL as an easy-stack type, so three identical horns
# merge into one pack entry. It is activated rather than consumed: blowing a
# horn is not meant to spend it, so an honest run must end with the same three
# horns it started with, which a potion or a scroll could never show. And its
# effect announces itself in one fixed sentence -- lib/m_items.irh gives the
# Plenty effect an EV_MAGIC_STRIKE message, "Fruits and produce appear at your
# feet!", and drops a pile of fresh food on the floor. Nothing about that
# message depends on a die roll, a monster or a saving throw, so the run needs
# no seed hunting. Seed 4 is used only because tools/check_horn_panic.sh
# already uses it.
#
# WIZARD MODE SUPPLIES THE HORNS, because no scripted walk can be relied on to
# find three of one magic item. [e] Item Acquisition opens
# TextTerm::AcquisitionPrompt (src/Term.cpp), which draws its candidates in the
# message window with the selected row in the middle and has NO menu letters,
# so @choose cannot reach a row. The idiom below is the one
# tools/keys/horn-panic.keys uses: RIGHT*28 walks the glyph strip to the Horns
# category, @until presses DOWN until the name appears at the bottom of that
# three-line window, and one further DOWN moves the selection onto it. Adding a
# horn effect to lib/ moves nothing here, because @until reads the name off the
# screen rather than counting rows -- but adding a glyph category before Horns
# WOULD break RIGHT*28, and then the run acquires something else. That is why
# tools/check_activate_stack.sh re-reads the item's name out of the game's own
# activate menu and calls a run that never held three horns INCONCLUSIVE.
#
# There is no quantity prompt in the acquisition list, so the block is written
# out three times. The two @while lines after each ENTER are insurance: an
# acquired item that will not stack lands in the In-the-Air slot and opens the
# inventory manager, which refuses to close while that slot is full. With these
# horns the game auto-stows instead and both lines fire zero times.
#
# Two menu dumps, and the comparison between them is the whole test:
#
#   menu-before   "[a] (in pack) 3 Horns of Plenty (3 uses left)"
#   blown         "Fruits and produce appear at your feet!"
#   menu-after    the same three horns. Item::Activate (src/Magic.cpp) split
#                 one unit off the stack with TakeOne() and never gave it back,
#                 so before the fix this line read TWO Horns of Plenty.
#
# The blown screen is not decoration. It is the half that fails if a "fix"
# keeps the stack whole by refusing to fire the effect at all.

@include chargen.keys

# Auto-Identify Items, so the horns arrive fully known and therefore identical
# to each other. Two units the game describes differently do not stack.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
DOWN
SPACE
ESC

# --- three Horns of Plenty --------------------------------------------------
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*28                # the Horns glyph category
@until "Horn of Plenty" DOWN
DOWN
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Horn" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*28
@until "Horn of Plenty" DOWN
DOWN
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Horn" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*28
@until "Horn of Plenty" DOWN
DOWN
ENTER
@while "In the Air':Horn" s
@while "Ready Hand" ESC

# --- blow one of the three --------------------------------------------------
# The activate menu is an ordinary LMenu and does carry letters, but its
# letters move: the pile of food this activation drops joins the list ahead of
# the horns, so the horns are [a] before and [b] after. @choose reads the
# letter the game printed rather than assuming one. Player::ItemMenu writes an
# item held in a container as "(in pack) 3 Horns of Plenty", so the count is
# part of the name, and a run in which the count is not 3 stops here instead of
# activating something else.
a
@dump:menu-before
@choose "(in pack) 3 Horns of Plenty"
@dump:blown

a
@dump:menu-after
ESC

@quit
